WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.364 --> 00:00:03.021
- If you think you can
have a balanced life

00:00:03.021 --> 00:00:05.078
as an entrepreneur,
that's just all bullshit.

00:00:05.078 --> 00:00:06.665
What I've learned about business

00:00:06.665 --> 00:00:09.239
over all these years and all these deals,

00:00:09.239 --> 00:00:11.283
it's never as good as it sounds,

00:00:11.283 --> 00:00:13.792
but it's never as bad as it looks either.

00:00:13.792 --> 00:00:15.909
♫ This land is your land

00:00:15.909 --> 00:00:17.930
♫ This land is my--

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:18.770
I screwed it up, let's start again.

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:19.830
(audience laughing)

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:20.663
- Hello believe nation,

00:00:20.663 --> 00:00:21.670
I'm Evan Carmichael.

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:23.263
My one word is believe

00:00:23.263 --> 00:00:25.764
and I believe that
entrepreneurs are going to solve

00:00:25.764 --> 00:00:28.061
all of the major problems of the world.

00:00:28.061 --> 00:00:29.423
So to help you on your journey,

00:00:29.423 --> 00:00:30.630
today we're going to learn from

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:33.095
Shark Tank investor, Kevin O'Leary,

00:00:33.095 --> 00:00:35.619
and my take on his Top
10 Rules For Success,

00:00:35.619 --> 00:00:36.812
Volume Two.

00:00:36.812 --> 00:00:38.365
Rule number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:38.365 --> 00:00:40.828
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:40.828 --> 00:00:42.264
And as always guys, as you're watching,

00:00:42.264 --> 00:00:44.916
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:44.916 --> 00:00:46.514
please leave it down the comments below,

00:00:46.514 --> 00:00:49.157
put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired,

00:00:49.157 --> 00:00:50.765
and when you write it down,

00:00:50.765 --> 00:00:53.871
it's much more likely to
stick for yourself as well.

00:00:53.871 --> 00:00:55.232
Enjoy.

00:00:55.232 --> 00:00:58.144
(cosmic whoosh)

00:00:58.144 --> 00:01:00.894
(dramatic music)

00:01:08.252 --> 00:01:10.611
- I was working once
at an ice-cream parlor

00:01:10.611 --> 00:01:12.870
when I was about 15 years old,

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.370
my first real job.

00:01:15.251 --> 00:01:17.735
And on the second day,

00:01:17.735 --> 00:01:20.082
I was asked to scrape the gum off

00:01:20.082 --> 00:01:21.789
between the Mexican tiles on the floor,

00:01:21.789 --> 00:01:23.349
and I said to the woman
who owned the store,

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:25.169
it was called Magoo's Ice-Cream Parlor,

00:01:25.169 --> 00:01:26.322
you didn't hire me to scrape gum,

00:01:26.322 --> 00:01:29.420
you hired me to scoop ice-cream.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.464
And this girl in the
shoe store across the way

00:01:31.464 --> 00:01:32.297
that's looking at me,

00:01:32.297 --> 00:01:35.157
I don't want her to see
me scraping the gum.

00:01:35.157 --> 00:01:37.342
And she said, "What are you talking about?

00:01:37.342 --> 00:01:38.394
"I own this store,

00:01:38.394 --> 00:01:40.184
"I'll do anything I want with you.

00:01:40.184 --> 00:01:42.329
"Get down on your knees
and scrape that gum."

00:01:42.329 --> 00:01:44.070
And I said, "I'm not doing it."

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.213
She said, "You're fired."

00:01:46.213 --> 00:01:48.073
Last job I ever had.

00:01:48.073 --> 00:01:49.101
(audience chuckles)

00:01:49.101 --> 00:01:51.434
I owe that woman everything.

00:01:53.212 --> 00:01:55.879
I don't like to work for people.

00:01:56.928 --> 00:02:00.261
Many of you in here I hope inherit that.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.289
There's nothing wrong with it.

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:05.718
If you want to control your own destiny,

00:02:05.718 --> 00:02:08.247
you work for yourself.

00:02:08.247 --> 00:02:10.010
That's how it works.

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:11.152
I'm trying to inspire those of you

00:02:11.152 --> 00:02:13.170
who are on the tipping point.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.718
Take the chance, you're young.

00:02:15.718 --> 00:02:16.551
Go for it.

00:02:17.501 --> 00:02:20.547
There's nothing better than being able

00:02:20.547 --> 00:02:22.665
to wake up in the morning,

00:02:22.665 --> 00:02:24.278
10 years or 20 years from now,

00:02:24.278 --> 00:02:26.361
and do whatever you like.

00:02:29.597 --> 00:02:31.703
It's about freedom.

00:02:31.703 --> 00:02:33.709
That's what matters.

00:02:33.709 --> 00:02:37.148
I'm not saying being an employee's bad,

00:02:37.148 --> 00:02:41.148
but believe me, being an
entrepreneur is better.

00:02:42.731 --> 00:02:43.923
I'm a little biased.

00:02:43.923 --> 00:02:45.022
(audience laughing)`

00:02:45.022 --> 00:02:47.490
Unfortunately, one of the great benefits

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:48.851
of technology's productivity.

00:02:48.851 --> 00:02:49.952
One of the big down sides,

00:02:49.952 --> 00:02:51.618
it's incredibly distracting.

00:02:51.618 --> 00:02:53.886
People check their Twitter account

00:02:53.886 --> 00:02:56.027
or they'll answer a Facebook message,

00:02:56.027 --> 00:02:58.843
or they'll do something
that has nothing to do

00:02:58.843 --> 00:03:00.432
with making money.

00:03:00.432 --> 00:03:02.094
And the reason you go
to work in the morning

00:03:02.094 --> 00:03:03.947
is to make money.

00:03:03.947 --> 00:03:05.083
That's what you're there for.

00:03:05.083 --> 00:03:07.129
All the other wonderful things
you can do with your time

00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:09.556
are based on your ability to
create an income for yourself

00:03:09.556 --> 00:03:10.879
and your family.

00:03:10.879 --> 00:03:14.353
So why spend any time doing all that crap,

00:03:14.353 --> 00:03:16.220
which is distracting?

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:17.869
And so that's the number one thing

00:03:17.869 --> 00:03:20.120
I find in my companies is

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:21.704
when you see someone failing

00:03:21.704 --> 00:03:24.745
and you look at how
they manage their time,

00:03:24.745 --> 00:03:26.577
you'll see right there what's going on.

00:03:26.577 --> 00:03:29.189
They're not focused on what matters.

00:03:29.189 --> 00:03:30.022
- Last time we were here

00:03:30.022 --> 00:03:31.336
we talked about your
meeting with Steve Jobs,

00:03:31.336 --> 00:03:33.282
and I read a lot about Steve
Jobs since he passed away,

00:03:33.282 --> 00:03:36.308
and people talk about he was
so unbelievably difficult

00:03:36.308 --> 00:03:37.660
and tough in a way.
- Yes he was.

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:39.771
- Would you characterize
him that way, yeah?

00:03:39.771 --> 00:03:41.395
- He was a prick.
- Yeah?

00:03:41.395 --> 00:03:42.405
(audience laughing)

00:03:42.405 --> 00:03:45.096
But do you have to be that way

00:03:45.096 --> 00:03:47.001
to accomplish what he's done?

00:03:47.001 --> 00:03:48.474
- You know, I respect him

00:03:48.474 --> 00:03:51.996
for the tremendous success
and the vision he had.

00:03:51.996 --> 00:03:53.483
I was proud to work for him,

00:03:53.483 --> 00:03:54.316
because that's what I was doing,

00:03:54.316 --> 00:03:55.888
supplying him educational software.

00:03:55.888 --> 00:03:57.572
I always thought in person

00:03:57.572 --> 00:03:59.203
he could've been a nicer guy,

00:03:59.203 --> 00:04:00.036
but would that have

00:04:00.036 --> 00:04:01.964
changed the outcome?
Maybe.

00:04:01.964 --> 00:04:03.357
He was a genius,

00:04:03.357 --> 00:04:05.503
and I remember him saying,

00:04:05.503 --> 00:04:07.436
I asked him about,
"Well, maybe we should do

00:04:07.436 --> 00:04:09.445
"some market research on this

00:04:09.445 --> 00:04:10.784
"before we spend two million dollars,"

00:04:10.784 --> 00:04:12.698
on a particular product we're
doing for Reader Rabbit.

00:04:12.698 --> 00:04:15.335
And he said, "We don't do market research.

00:04:15.335 --> 00:04:17.449
"We tell them what they want."

00:04:17.449 --> 00:04:18.571
and I thought wow,

00:04:18.571 --> 00:04:20.904
not only is that arrogant,

00:04:20.904 --> 00:04:22.190
but it's brilliant.
- Yeah.

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:23.215
(audience laughing)
- And it worked.

00:04:23.215 --> 00:04:24.701
- Right.
- And so again I said,

00:04:24.701 --> 00:04:25.840
"Okay big guy, let's do it."

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:27.841
And it was very successful.

00:04:27.841 --> 00:04:30.654
That whatever you do,
somebody can do it better.

00:04:30.654 --> 00:04:34.337
You have to bring your
A-game in every single day.

00:04:34.337 --> 00:04:37.376
There's nothing unique
about any one individual.

00:04:37.376 --> 00:04:39.126
What's unique is their passion,

00:04:39.126 --> 00:04:41.260
their ability to execute,

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:43.828
to meet targets and exceed them.

00:04:43.828 --> 00:04:46.578
The minute you lose that ability,

00:04:48.089 --> 00:04:50.339
in capitalism, you're gone.

00:04:51.423 --> 00:04:53.705
It's a fair trade.

00:04:53.705 --> 00:04:55.748
The engine that makes you rich

00:04:55.748 --> 00:04:58.038
can take it all away from you.

00:04:58.038 --> 00:05:00.628
There are no guarantees.

00:05:00.628 --> 00:05:03.278
I think that's incredibly important.

00:05:03.278 --> 00:05:04.624
- What's a deal that you missed

00:05:04.624 --> 00:05:07.129
that one of your fellow
sharks was able to secure

00:05:07.129 --> 00:05:08.973
that you look back on and say,

00:05:08.973 --> 00:05:11.175
"Wow, I didn't know that one
was going to work out that well,

00:05:11.175 --> 00:05:12.831
"but it's proven to be a big success."?

00:05:12.831 --> 00:05:13.664
- And there is one,

00:05:13.664 --> 00:05:15.828
and I remember the moment.

00:05:15.828 --> 00:05:17.396
It was that deal called Sponge Daddy.

00:05:17.396 --> 00:05:21.680
It was a yellow sponge
with a smile in its face.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:24.334
And I thought, "What a
piece of crap this is.

00:05:24.334 --> 00:05:28.749
The guy wanted $500,000
for, like, 50% of it.

00:05:28.749 --> 00:05:30.529
And Laurie was beside me,

00:05:30.529 --> 00:05:33.016
you know, the queen of
crap, as I like to say.

00:05:33.016 --> 00:05:35.019
She'd get the joke if she was here.

00:05:35.019 --> 00:05:37.965
And she started bidding on it,

00:05:37.965 --> 00:05:38.917
and I looked over at her and I said,

00:05:38.917 --> 00:05:39.916
"Are you out of your mind?

00:05:39.916 --> 00:05:41.937
"This thing is a sponge.

00:05:41.937 --> 00:05:42.770
"It's a sponge."

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:43.631
She said, "Leave me alone."

00:05:43.631 --> 00:05:46.191
I said, "No, Laurie, I
forbid you to do this.

00:05:46.191 --> 00:05:48.281
"I can't watch you kill money like this.

00:05:48.281 --> 00:05:49.114
"This is a shame.

00:05:49.114 --> 00:05:50.954
"You're going to teach
America how to waste cash."

00:05:50.954 --> 00:05:52.635
She said, "Shut up, I'm doing the deal."

00:05:52.635 --> 00:05:54.298
And she did.

00:05:54.298 --> 00:05:57.746
Sponge Daddy did $50
million in sales last year.

00:05:57.746 --> 00:06:00.089
It's a little piece of crap.

00:06:00.089 --> 00:06:01.832
I mean, that taught me a--

00:06:01.832 --> 00:06:04.392
and I have a lot of respect for her now.

00:06:04.392 --> 00:06:08.031
That shows you the power
of the Shark Tank platform.

00:06:08.031 --> 00:06:10.818
Simple product, simple message.

00:06:10.818 --> 00:06:13.404
There's actually a great
corporate lesson to be learned

00:06:13.404 --> 00:06:15.987
in watching Shark Tank.

00:06:15.987 --> 00:06:17.680
And I urge anybody that's
listening to this now

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:19.892
to think about this for their own career.

00:06:19.892 --> 00:06:21.779
If you go back and look
at six years of data,

00:06:21.779 --> 00:06:23.474
of all the deals that got funded,

00:06:23.474 --> 00:06:25.151
regardless to what the product was.

00:06:25.151 --> 00:06:27.456
This does not detail the
outcome of the company,

00:06:27.456 --> 00:06:30.617
just the ones that go
a check from a shark.

00:06:30.617 --> 00:06:32.916
What are the common traits or threads?

00:06:32.916 --> 00:06:34.563
It turns out there's three.

00:06:34.563 --> 00:06:37.017
In 100% of the cases,
the entrepreneur was able

00:06:37.017 --> 00:06:40.035
to articulate the idea
in 90 seconds or less.

00:06:40.035 --> 00:06:40.868
Here's a sponge.

00:06:40.868 --> 00:06:43.369
Under cold water it's hard,
scrapes eggs off plates,

00:06:43.369 --> 00:06:45.367
under warm water you can polish silver.

00:06:45.367 --> 00:06:47.906
I get it, right?
Very simple.

00:06:47.906 --> 00:06:50.518
Number two: it's usually a team

00:06:50.518 --> 00:06:52.629
are able to articulate
why they're right team

00:06:52.629 --> 00:06:54.333
to execute the business plan.

00:06:54.333 --> 00:06:55.483
They have a plan.

00:06:55.483 --> 00:06:56.975
They tell you what it
is and they tell you why

00:06:56.975 --> 00:07:00.011
they're the accredited managers for it.

00:07:00.011 --> 00:07:00.844
That's interesting.

00:07:00.844 --> 00:07:02.936
You find it in every case with a success.

00:07:02.936 --> 00:07:05.791
And here's the one that really matters.

00:07:05.791 --> 00:07:07.812
In 100% of the cases,

00:07:07.812 --> 00:07:10.551
the entrepreneurs knew
their numbers inside out.

00:07:10.551 --> 00:07:12.403
If you would ask them
about break even analysis,

00:07:12.403 --> 00:07:16.129
margin analysis, market
share growth, competition,

00:07:16.129 --> 00:07:19.386
in every answer they
had it, they nailed it.

00:07:19.386 --> 00:07:21.519
It turns out in corporate America today,

00:07:21.519 --> 00:07:22.468
and I teach this now,

00:07:22.468 --> 00:07:24.853
you know, I taught at Norte Dame, at MIT,

00:07:24.853 --> 00:07:27.120
the graduating cohorts,
I explained this to them.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.342
When you're leading, when
you want to be a leader,

00:07:29.342 --> 00:07:31.813
you have to articulate your
idea in 90 seconds or less.

00:07:31.813 --> 00:07:34.562
You have to explain why you're
the right person to lead,

00:07:34.562 --> 00:07:37.269
and above all you have
to know your numbers.

00:07:37.269 --> 00:07:38.907
You can see it happen on Shark Tank.

00:07:38.907 --> 00:07:41.570
It works in the real world too.

00:07:41.570 --> 00:07:43.789
And frankly Shark Tank is the real world

00:07:43.789 --> 00:07:45.041
because it's real capital.

00:07:45.041 --> 00:07:46.986
So I use that and I teach it,

00:07:46.986 --> 00:07:48.853
and I think everybody
should think about that.

00:07:48.853 --> 00:07:52.129
Explaining your vision
clearly, succinctly,

00:07:52.129 --> 00:07:56.072
in a short period of time ends
up being really important.

00:07:56.072 --> 00:07:59.731
If you think you can have a
balanced life as an entrepreneur

00:07:59.731 --> 00:08:01.765
that's just all bullshit.

00:08:01.765 --> 00:08:04.818
There's some other guy
who wants your customer

00:08:04.818 --> 00:08:06.932
that's working 25 hours a day.
- Right.

00:08:06.932 --> 00:08:07.765
- That's what I found,

00:08:07.765 --> 00:08:11.846
and so I don't even talk
about that any more,

00:08:11.846 --> 00:08:13.390
saying look, it's a great--

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:14.460
You have to decide.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:15.377
It's a path.

00:08:15.377 --> 00:08:18.384
You either are going to
control your own destiny,

00:08:18.384 --> 00:08:19.234
or you're going to be an employee.

00:08:19.234 --> 00:08:21.188
They're both noble.

00:08:21.188 --> 00:08:22.765
One gives you time to be with your family,

00:08:22.765 --> 00:08:24.053
one gives you a more balanced life,

00:08:24.053 --> 00:08:27.288
and the other one is balls to the wall,

00:08:27.288 --> 00:08:28.747
and the reason you do it is

00:08:28.747 --> 00:08:29.961
to eventually have personal freedom

00:08:29.961 --> 00:08:31.426
because you've been successful.

00:08:31.426 --> 00:08:32.941
And I think most of
the people in this room

00:08:32.941 --> 00:08:34.742
fall into that second category,

00:08:34.742 --> 00:08:35.658
balls to the walls,

00:08:35.658 --> 00:08:37.384
and they're going to
make it or they're not,

00:08:37.384 --> 00:08:39.963
and that's why I come here,

00:08:39.963 --> 00:08:41.186
'cause I was a kindred spirit.

00:08:41.186 --> 00:08:43.872
I like to see entrepreneurs
that are willing to risk it all,

00:08:43.872 --> 00:08:46.781
but have to realize there is no balance.

00:08:46.781 --> 00:08:50.455
The reason why you do and
pursue an entrepreneurial career

00:08:50.455 --> 00:08:53.412
is to provide financial freedom one day

00:08:53.412 --> 00:08:55.423
for yourself and your family.

00:08:55.423 --> 00:08:57.044
The sacrifice is there's no balance

00:08:57.044 --> 00:08:58.592
during the period that
you're growing your business.

00:08:58.592 --> 00:08:59.900
If you're successful,

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:01.683
the reward is financial freedom,

00:09:01.683 --> 00:09:03.280
which is the essence.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:04.694
I tell the entrepreneurs,

00:09:04.694 --> 00:09:06.272
"Don't do it for the greed of money.

00:09:06.272 --> 00:09:08.122
"Do it for financial freedom.

00:09:08.122 --> 00:09:08.955
"Do it for freedom.

00:09:08.955 --> 00:09:09.869
"Make yourself free one day

00:09:09.869 --> 00:09:11.839
"so you can do whatever
you wish with your time."

00:09:11.839 --> 00:09:13.411
And the only way you can
do that in our society

00:09:13.411 --> 00:09:15.333
is you have to be wealthy to do that.

00:09:15.333 --> 00:09:18.762
And so the rewards of a
great entrepreneur are

00:09:18.762 --> 00:09:19.824
they achieve that wealth and they're free

00:09:19.824 --> 00:09:20.913
to do whatever they wish,

00:09:20.913 --> 00:09:22.433
which may be more entrepreneurship.

00:09:22.433 --> 00:09:23.695
You don't know.

00:09:23.695 --> 00:09:26.175
If you're passionate
and you're successful,

00:09:26.175 --> 00:09:29.338
you will wake up one day rich.

00:09:29.338 --> 00:09:31.040
You won't really be going for the cash,

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:32.554
it'll just happen to you.

00:09:32.554 --> 00:09:35.082
Everybody I talk to that's had success

00:09:35.082 --> 00:09:39.010
doesn't remember the day
they became a millionaire.

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:39.843
No idea.

00:09:40.686 --> 00:09:42.908
Not important to them.

00:09:42.908 --> 00:09:44.479
They remember winning.

00:09:44.479 --> 00:09:45.664
They remember building a business

00:09:45.664 --> 00:09:47.414
and being successful.

00:09:48.962 --> 00:09:51.142
Again, the pursuit of freedom

00:09:51.142 --> 00:09:53.297
manifests itself in passion.

00:09:53.297 --> 00:09:55.216
That's really what it's about.

00:09:55.216 --> 00:09:57.419
- And to my exposure,

00:09:57.419 --> 00:09:59.909
most of the business people I've met,

00:09:59.909 --> 00:10:02.629
the successful ones, the CEOs or whatever,

00:10:02.629 --> 00:10:05.462
they are at their core optimistic.

00:10:06.642 --> 00:10:10.809
Can you be a pessimist and
be a successful entrepreneur?

00:10:12.044 --> 00:10:14.338
You have to be optimistic?

00:10:14.338 --> 00:10:17.035
- I think you have to view the world

00:10:17.035 --> 00:10:20.348
in a way that assumes success,

00:10:20.348 --> 00:10:23.022
otherwise you'll never have the energy.

00:10:23.022 --> 00:10:25.368
Maybe not a pessimist,

00:10:25.368 --> 00:10:28.022
but a skeptic is a good thing to be,

00:10:28.022 --> 00:10:31.862
because what I've learned about business

00:10:31.862 --> 00:10:34.065
over all these years and all these deals,

00:10:34.065 --> 00:10:36.393
it's never as good as it sounds

00:10:36.393 --> 00:10:39.906
but it's never as bad as it looks either.

00:10:39.906 --> 00:10:44.308
Every day, I've got 32
portfolio companies now,

00:10:44.308 --> 00:10:47.402
something wildly bad is
happening to one of them,

00:10:47.402 --> 00:10:49.490
and the cellphone rings
at six in the morning,

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:51.415
and something fantastically great

00:10:51.415 --> 00:10:52.842
is happening to another one.

00:10:52.842 --> 00:10:54.425
And it seems to me,

00:10:55.269 --> 00:10:58.813
the random serendipitous nature of it all

00:10:58.813 --> 00:11:00.451
is really intriguing to me,

00:11:00.451 --> 00:11:03.129
because if I could only
show the pessimistic

00:11:03.129 --> 00:11:06.046
or very depressed entrepreneur that

00:11:07.190 --> 00:11:08.727
is calling me saying, "Oh my goodness,

00:11:08.727 --> 00:11:10.907
"we just lost Walmart," or whatever it is,

00:11:10.907 --> 00:11:12.679
it's the nightmare of their day,

00:11:12.679 --> 00:11:16.058
versus, "We just got Walmart,"
on the other business.

00:11:16.058 --> 00:11:17.662
Well I'm trying to get
everybody in the same universe

00:11:17.662 --> 00:11:20.705
to realize it's never as bad as it sounds,

00:11:20.705 --> 00:11:22.285
but never as good either.

00:11:22.285 --> 00:11:24.102
You have to get up in the
morning and go back to work.

00:11:24.102 --> 00:11:26.890
Great entrepreneurs are able to delegate.

00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:28.582
The successful women CEOs

00:11:28.582 --> 00:11:30.923
are remarkably good at this.

00:11:30.923 --> 00:11:32.657
Remarkably good at it.

00:11:32.657 --> 00:11:33.822
And the way it works is,

00:11:33.822 --> 00:11:35.050
Sarah, I'll give you an example

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:36.182
of the way Sarah works.

00:11:36.182 --> 00:11:37.092
She hires somebody,

00:11:37.092 --> 00:11:38.519
she sets a goal for 90 days,

00:11:38.519 --> 00:11:40.627
and gets out of their way.

00:11:40.627 --> 00:11:43.098
Gets completely out of their way.

00:11:43.098 --> 00:11:44.084
Lets them achieve it.

00:11:44.084 --> 00:11:46.268
It they achieve it she moves
them up the organization.

00:11:46.268 --> 00:11:49.506
If they don't, they get three
tries until they're out.

00:11:49.506 --> 00:11:51.728
If you can't achieve
your goals in business,

00:11:51.728 --> 00:11:53.030
that's what happens.

00:11:53.030 --> 00:11:54.570
But her whole thing is

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:55.585
let them do the work themselves,

00:11:55.585 --> 00:11:56.658
let them figure it out.

00:11:56.658 --> 00:11:58.047
I don't want to use my time for it,

00:11:58.047 --> 00:11:59.531
that's what I hired them for.

00:11:59.531 --> 00:12:01.127
What do you want to play?
(plucks guitar)

00:12:01.127 --> 00:12:02.092
See, if I think--

00:12:02.092 --> 00:12:03.283
- Kevin O'Leary, you know what I want

00:12:03.283 --> 00:12:05.042
you to play for me?
- (strums guitar)

00:12:05.042 --> 00:12:07.377
- I want you to sing
This Land Is Your Land.

00:12:07.377 --> 00:12:08.635
- Ooh.
(audience laughing)

00:12:08.635 --> 00:12:12.802
- The classic, pro-labor,
political subversive song.

00:12:15.009 --> 00:12:16.386
We got to bring the lyrics--
- I got to tell ya,

00:12:16.386 --> 00:12:17.590
'cause I got to have the lyrics,

00:12:17.590 --> 00:12:18.708
'cause I'd never sing this thing.

00:12:18.708 --> 00:12:20.368
- (laughs) I know, that sounds good.

00:12:20.368 --> 00:12:22.673
- This is absolutely
against everything I am.

00:12:22.673 --> 00:12:24.216
- [Host] I know (laughs).

00:12:24.216 --> 00:12:25.362
(audience laughing)

00:12:25.362 --> 00:12:26.212
(strumming guitar)

00:12:26.212 --> 00:12:28.103
- Remember this tune?

00:12:28.103 --> 00:12:29.895
Starts with a G.

00:12:29.895 --> 00:12:31.645
Ready?
We're going to go.

00:12:33.257 --> 00:12:34.980
♫ This land is your land

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:36.591
♫ This land is my--

00:12:36.591 --> 00:12:37.931
I screwed it up, let's start again.

00:12:37.931 --> 00:12:38.764
(audience laughing)

00:12:38.764 --> 00:12:39.597
That's how much I hate

00:12:39.597 --> 00:12:41.909
this tune.
- It sounds good.

00:12:41.909 --> 00:12:44.074
That's all it is, right?
♫ This land is your land

00:12:44.074 --> 00:12:46.111
♫ This land is my land

00:12:46.111 --> 00:12:47.783
♫ From California

00:12:47.783 --> 00:12:49.745
♫ To the New York island

00:12:49.745 --> 00:12:51.831
♫ Cape (unclear)

00:12:51.831 --> 00:12:52.728
- Ooh, I hate this tune.

00:12:52.728 --> 00:12:54.978
- (laughs)

00:12:58.045 --> 00:12:59.159
- All right.
♫ This land is your land

00:12:59.159 --> 00:13:01.284
- Okay, go, go.
- Let's try it again.

00:13:01.284 --> 00:13:02.269
I want you to sing with me.

00:13:02.269 --> 00:13:03.102
- Dude, are you crazy?
- You're not

00:13:03.102 --> 00:13:04.708
getting away with this.
- All right.

00:13:04.708 --> 00:13:05.616
(audience laughing)
- All right,

00:13:05.616 --> 00:13:06.449
here we go.

00:13:06.449 --> 00:13:07.808
♫ This land is your land

00:13:07.808 --> 00:13:09.845
♫ This land is my land

00:13:09.845 --> 00:13:11.888
♫ From California

00:13:11.888 --> 00:13:14.206
♫ To the New York islands

00:13:14.206 --> 00:13:16.200
♫ Blah blah-blah blah blah blah

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:18.159
♫ To the Gulf Stream waters
- Here we go.

00:13:18.159 --> 00:13:18.992
- Everybody!

00:13:18.992 --> 00:13:23.159
♫ This land was made for you and me

00:13:25.316 --> 00:13:26.528
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:26.528 --> 00:13:27.569
I hope you enjoyed.

00:13:27.569 --> 00:13:31.060
If you want to get your pick
of who I should profile next

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:32.338
in the Top Tens,

00:13:32.338 --> 00:13:33.813
check out the link in the description

00:13:33.813 --> 00:13:35.192
and go and cast your vote.

00:13:35.192 --> 00:13:37.670
I also want to give a quick
shout-out to Amy Schmittauer.

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:40.478
Amy, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:13:40.478 --> 00:13:41.311
Your One Word,

00:13:41.311 --> 00:13:44.043
and doing that awesome
podcast on your channel,

00:13:44.043 --> 00:13:45.262
and you Tweeting it out.

00:13:45.262 --> 00:13:46.944
I appreciate all the support,

00:13:46.944 --> 00:13:48.456
and I hope you enjoyed the read.

00:13:48.456 --> 00:13:49.679
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:13:49.679 --> 00:13:50.894
I believe in you.

00:13:50.894 --> 00:13:52.964
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:13:52.964 --> 00:13:55.492
and whatever your one word is.

00:13:55.492 --> 00:13:56.806
Much love.

00:13:56.806 --> 00:13:58.175
I'll see you soon.

00:13:58.175 --> 00:14:00.842
(cosmic whoosh)

00:14:04.339 --> 00:14:06.977
- Great entrepreneurs have a
tremendous amount of knowledge

00:14:06.977 --> 00:14:08.435
of what they're selling.

00:14:08.435 --> 00:14:10.234
They know a lot about everything,

00:14:10.234 --> 00:14:13.851
but they better know a lot
about what they're selling.

00:14:13.851 --> 00:14:16.569
You never want to be
caught explaining yourself

00:14:16.569 --> 00:14:18.404
and not knowing your product.

00:14:18.404 --> 00:14:19.691
That's because you're so passionate

00:14:19.691 --> 00:14:21.238
you're spending all your time doing it.

00:14:21.238 --> 00:14:23.407
That's exactly what matters.

00:14:23.407 --> 00:14:25.656
I actually think what
motivates entrepreneurs

00:14:25.656 --> 00:14:26.992
is fear of failure,

00:14:26.992 --> 00:14:28.180
and this certainly happened to me.

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:29.547
You start a deal, it's not working,

00:14:29.547 --> 00:14:30.942
you work harder to make it work,

00:14:30.942 --> 00:14:32.992
you pivot, you figure out
what you're doing wrong.

00:14:32.992 --> 00:14:36.867
Fear of failure is the
ultimate motivation,

00:14:36.867 --> 00:14:37.975
and I think many--

00:14:37.975 --> 00:14:39.397
Every entrepreneur has failures.

00:14:39.397 --> 00:14:42.397
Your job is to go out into the world

00:14:43.626 --> 00:14:46.163
and understand who you serve.

00:14:46.163 --> 00:14:47.823
It is not your responsibility

00:14:47.823 --> 00:14:50.906
to solve all the societies' problems.

00:14:52.471 --> 00:14:55.871
You pay taxes to a government to do that.

00:14:55.871 --> 00:14:56.704
Your job

00:14:58.754 --> 00:15:01.587
is to go to war every day and win.

00:15:02.711 --> 00:15:04.510
To win market share.

00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:06.885
To salt the earth your
competitors walk on.

00:15:06.885 --> 00:15:08.457
To steal their customers,

00:15:08.457 --> 00:15:11.290
and to take their profits and win.

00:15:12.372 --> 00:15:14.188
That's your job.

00:15:14.188 --> 00:15:16.957
And to make profits for your shareholders

00:15:16.957 --> 00:15:20.223
so they give you more
capital to create more jobs,

00:15:20.223 --> 00:15:23.687
to be more successful, to pay more taxes,

00:15:23.687 --> 00:15:26.117
to provide the standard of living

00:15:26.117 --> 00:15:28.617
that we have in America today.

00:15:29.743 --> 00:15:32.183
Your job is not to solve
everybody's problem.

00:15:32.183 --> 00:15:34.900
It's to solve your customer's problems,

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:36.331
your shareholders problems,

00:15:36.331 --> 00:15:38.796
your employees problems.

00:15:38.796 --> 00:15:42.359
I'm very distressed when people tell me

00:15:42.359 --> 00:15:46.318
a business should give a third
of it's profits to charity.

00:15:46.318 --> 00:15:48.280
That's outrageous.

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:50.484
It should give all of
it to its shareholders

00:15:50.484 --> 00:15:52.230
who then give to charity.

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:54.623
Think of all the great
things that are done today

00:15:54.623 --> 00:15:56.144
by the Bill Gates of the world,

00:15:56.144 --> 00:15:58.231
the Warren Buffets of the world.

00:15:58.231 --> 00:15:59.385
The only reason they can do that

00:15:59.385 --> 00:16:02.890
is they make money for their shareholders.

00:16:02.890 --> 00:16:05.390
Don't get lost in this debate.

00:16:06.808 --> 00:16:10.083
Stay focused and true to the mission.

00:16:10.083 --> 00:16:11.416
Business is war.

00:16:12.353 --> 00:16:13.686
You have to win.

00:16:14.915 --> 00:16:17.111
And you take the spoils which are profits,

00:16:17.111 --> 00:16:20.197
and you help your fellow man.

00:16:20.197 --> 00:16:22.280
But first you make money.

